# The fusions that owe an exact composed remainder. One row per composed layer whose demand and
# nameplate eliminations are both answered, so `r_composed = F Phi r_parts - e_n + e_d` is a closed
# expression rather than a warning.
#
# A difference whose left operand is a file
#   That is the point. A difference whose left side is spelled inline cannot be counted, so no law
#   on algebra/roster.sqlc can govern it. `composition/fusions.sqlc` is the A,
#   `composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc` is the B, and algebra/owed_remainder.sqlc counts them.
#   See composition/carriable.sqlc, which exists for the same reason at the other difference.
#
# The remainder's twin of the demand's
#   It is the remainder's twin of composition/owed_equality.sqlc, which is the demand's. That one
#   suspends on `coalesce(s.quantity, 'demand') = 'demand'` alone. A remainder needs both arms, so
#   the two populations differ and neither substitutes for the other.
#
# It makes `coalesce(e, 0)` sound one join later
#   Once the suspended fusions are out, an absent elimination means the composer looked and found
#   no double counting, which is the number zero. Inside the suspended set the same absence means
#   nobody looked, and filling it would put a claim in the checker that the document declined to
#   make.
-- composition/fusions.sqlc less composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT f.filing, f.layer
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc)
) s ON s.composition = f.filing AND s.composed_layer = f.layer
WHERE s.composition IS NULL
